course: everyday
lesson: 309
title: Respecting the festivals and traditions of classmates
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A class in London, Toronto or Sydney often has children from many families, religions and cultures. Over a year, classmates may celebrate Christmas, Diwali, Eid, Hanukkah, Lunar New Year and other festivals, and some families celebrate none of them.
explain: Everyone's traditions deserve respect. Respect means being curious and kind: asking polite questions, saying «Happy Diwali» or «Eid Mubarak», and never laughing at someone's food, clothes or customs.
explain: Example: during Ramadan a girl in Birmingham fasts from dawn to sunset. Her friends do not wave snacks in her face or tease her; they ask what the fast means to her, and at Eid they wish her a happy festival.
explain: You do not have to share a belief to show respect for it. Respecting other people's festivals is how everyone's own traditions are respected too.
fact: Everyone's traditions deserve respect, even when they are different from yours.
fact: Christmas is a Christian festival on 25 December that celebrates the birth of Jesus.
fact: Diwali is the festival of lights, celebrated by Hindus, Sikhs and Jains with lamps, sweets and fireworks.
fact: Eid al-Fitr is the Muslim festival that ends Ramadan, a month of fasting from dawn to sunset.
fact: Hanukkah is a Jewish festival of lights that lasts eight nights, with a candle lit on the menorah each night.
fact: Lunar New Year is celebrated by Chinese, Korean, Vietnamese and other families, often with red envelopes, family meals and lion dances.
fact: Diwali, Eid, Hanukkah and Lunar New Year fall on different dates each year because they follow lunar or lunisolar calendars (calendars that follow the Moon, or the Moon and the Sun together).
fact: Showing respect means asking polite questions, using kind greetings and never mocking someone's food, clothes or customs.
fact: Some families do not celebrate certain festivals, and that choice deserves respect too.
fact: You do not have to share a belief to be respectful about it.
exercise: A classmate tells the class how her family celebrates Diwali. What is the respectful thing to do? | Listen with interest and perhaps ask a kind question | Say loudly that your own festival is much better and much more fun | Laugh at the clothes in her photos | Talk to your friend until she has finished
exercise: Which festival is known as the festival of lights and is celebrated by Hindus, Sikhs and Jains? | Diwali | Hanukkah | Lunar New Year | Eid al-Fitr
exercise: What does Eid al-Fitr mark? | The end of Ramadan, the month of fasting | The beginning of the school year | The shortest day of the year | The birth of Jesus
exercise: A boy is fasting during Ramadan. What is the kind way to behave at lunchtime? | Be friendly and do not tease him about not eating | Wave your snacks in front of him at the table, just as a friendly joke | Tell him fasting is silly and he should eat | Ignore him for the whole month
exercise: How long does Hanukkah last? | Eight nights | One afternoon | Forty days | A single night
exercise: What do many families give children at Lunar New Year? | Red envelopes with money inside | Chocolate eggs hidden in the garden | Lit pumpkins with carved faces | Small paper hats from crackers
exercise: Why does the date of Eid or Diwali change from year to year? | They follow lunar or lunisolar calendars, not the usual one | Families choose a new date each year that suits their holidays and work | The government moves them every year | The dates are picked at random by schools
exercise: Christmas celebrates the birth of whom? | Jesus | Moses | The Buddha | The prophet Muhammad
exercise: A new boy brings food from home that smells different from yours. What is respectful? | Say nothing unkind, or ask politely what it is | Hold your nose and say it smells bad | Tell other children not to sit near him | Ask the teacher to make him bring a cheese sandwich like everybody else
exercise: A classmate's family does not celebrate birthdays or certain festivals. What is right? | Respect their choice and include them kindly | Tell them they are missing out and must join in | Leave them out of everything in class | Keep asking until they give a reason
exercise: Do you have to believe in a religion to be respectful of its festival? | No, you can respect it without sharing the belief | Yes, only believers may be respectful | Yes, otherwise you must stay silent | No, because respect is only for your own festival
exercise: What is a kind thing to say to a Muslim classmate at Eid? | «Eid Mubarak!» | «Merry Halloween!» | «Why do you even do that?» | «Happy birthday!»
test: Which is true about the festivals in a class in Toronto? | Different children may celebrate different festivals, and all deserve respect | Only the festivals of the biggest group in the class deserve respect at school | Everyone must celebrate the same festivals | It is rude to mention festivals at school at all
test: A girl laughs at a menorah in a classmate's photo. What could you tell her? | It is part of Hanukkah, and laughing at someone's tradition is unkind | It is just a strange lamp, so laughing is fine | She should only laugh when the classmate is not looking | Menorahs are only used at Christmas, so the photo must be wrong and she can laugh
test: A boy in Manchester says Diwali was in November last year but will not be in November every year. Why is he right? | Diwali follows a lunisolar calendar, so its date moves | Diwali is always held on 25 December | Diwali happens only once every ten years | Each family picks any date it likes for Diwali, and his family chose November
test: Your friend is fasting in Ramadan and you are going for ice cream after school. What would be thoughtful? | Invite her another day, or ask her what she would like to do | Eat the ice cream slowly right in front of her, so she can see what she's missing | Tell her fasting is pointless and she should come | Tell everyone she is boring this month
test: Which pair is correct? | Hanukkah – a Jewish festival of lights | Eid al-Fitr – a Christian festival in December | Lunar New Year – the end of Ramadan | Christmas – a festival of eight nights
test: A classmate from Vietnam tells you about Tết, the Lunar New Year. What is the best reply? | «That sounds interesting! What do you eat?» | «That's not a real new year, the real one is in January.» | «New Year is only on 1 January.» | «Nobody wants to hear about that.»
test: Why is respecting other people's traditions good for you too? | When everyone respects each other, your own traditions are respected as well | It means you never have to celebrate anything | It lets you skip school on every festival | It makes your own festival more important than everyone else's, so you come first
test: A child does not join the class Christmas card-making because their family does not celebrate it. What is true? | Their choice deserves respect, and they can be included in another way | They must make a Christmas card like everyone, or be sent out of the class | They are being rude to the teacher | Everyone should keep asking them why not
test: my mate says diwali is just for christmas lights. no?? | No, it's the Hindu, Sikh and Jain festival of lights | Yes, it's the bit of Christmas where everyone puts up the lights | Yes, it's an American holiday | No, it's a Jewish festival
test: why's eid on a different day every year | It follows a lunar calendar | The date is picked by each family | It moves to the nearest weekend | It's always on the same day really
test: kid in class says his family doesnt do birthdays | Respect it, families choose what they celebrate | Tell him that's weird | Make him come to your party so he sees what he's missing out on | Tell the teacher he's being rude
test: hanukkah how many nights again, 7? | Eight, one candle more each night | Seven, like the days of the week | Twelve, like Christmas | One big night only
explain: A boy in Leeds laughed at a classmate's Eid clothes; the classmate went home in tears and did not want to come to school the next day.
explain: A girl in Canada asked her Hindu friend about Diwali; she was invited to share the sweets and the lamps, and they became better friends.
fact: Being mocked for your religion or traditions can make children feel unsafe at school.
fact: Asking politely about a festival usually brings friendship, while mocking brings hurt.
fact: If others mock someone's religion, festival or clothes, tell them to stop and tell a teacher.
test: whys he not eating at lunch, its weird | He may be fasting for Ramadan; be kind, not mean | He's just being silly and showing off, ignore him | Make him eat | Tease him till he eats
test: they're laughing at her headscarf | Tell them to stop and tell a teacher | Laugh too to fit in | Pull it to see what's under | Say nothing, it's not your business
test: friend's family does hanukkah not christmas, is that wrong | No, every family's traditions deserve respect | Yes, everyone should do Christmas | Yes, it's strange | Only if they live abroad
test: did u hear some kids mocked a girl's Eid clothes and she "was too scared to come to school for a week". lesson? | Never mock someone's festival clothes; it makes them feel unsafe | Mocking is fine if it's a joke | Only mock clothes you don't like | Festival clothes should stay at home
test: my cousin says Diwali fireworks "set a whole street on fire last year". what's prob exaggerated | The whole street; Diwali is celebrated with lamps, sweets and fireworks | That Diwali has lights, like the little clay lamps people put out | That people light lamps | That it is a festival
test: kids say the family that doesn't do Christmas "must hate fun". pass it on? | No, families' choices deserve respect; don't spread it | Yes, they hate fun | Yes, and invite everyone but them | Yes, and ask them in front of the class
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises Eid al-Fitr? | The Muslim festival that ends the month of Ramadan | A Jewish festival of eight nights, with a candle lit on each | A Hindu festival of lights | A Christian festival in December
exam: Identify the principal reason for which festivals such as Diwali, Eid and Hanukkah fall on different dates each year. | They follow lunar or lunisolar calendars | They are fixed by each school | They depend upon the weather | They are chosen by each family
exam: It is given that a classmate celebrates a festival unfamiliar to a pupil. Select the response consistent with respect. | The pupil may ask polite questions and use kind greetings | The pupil shall ignore the festival entirely | The pupil shall mock the unfamiliar food | The pupil shall insist that the classmate explain it publicly
exam: State which of the following is true of Hanukkah. | A Jewish festival of lights lasting eight nights | A Muslim festival following a month of fasting, with gifts of sweets | A Christian festival upon 25 December | A Chinese festival with red envelopes
